# Never tell the operator to connect a pin to a voltage nobody has measured

**Seven GPIO pads have been destroyed across two RAK4631 cores**
([#102](https://github.com/disruptivepatternmaterial/rak-sensor-node-but-better/issues/102)).
Every one was the pad carrying the RAK9154's data line. Every one was connected on an agent's
instruction. And the voltage on that data line **has never been measured** — not once, on any
node, in the whole life of this project. It was assumed to be 3.3 V because pin 4 is labelled
`3V3_In`, and a label is not a measurement.

That is the defect this rule exists to stop. Not a wiring mistake — a **reasoning** mistake that
agents keep repeating: treating an unverified electrical level as a known one, and spending the
operator's hardware to find out.

## The asymmetry that governs every probe decision

| Instrument | Survives | Source |
|---|---|---|
| nRF52840 GPIO pad, powered | `VDD + 0.3 V` ≈ **3.6 V** | [CIT-NRF-GPIO] |
| nRF52840 GPIO pad, **unpowered** | **0.3 V** | [CIT-NRF-BACKPOWER] |
| Saleae Logic Pro 8 input | **−25 V to +25 V** continuous, ±10 V analog range, 2 MΩ | [CIT-SALEAE-LOGICPRO8] |

The analyzer tolerates roughly **seven times** what the pad tolerates, and loads the line with
2 MΩ instead of an ESD diode. **So the analyzer always goes first.** There is no measurement worth
taking that justifies risking a core to take it, and there never was.

## Forbidden instructions

Do not tell the operator to do any of these. Not as a suggestion, not as a next step, not
hedged, not "just to test":

- **Connect any wire to any nRF52840 pad when that wire's voltage has not been measured or
  datasheet-sourced.** The pack's data line is the live example: unmeasured to this day.
- **Connect a pad to any rail above 3.3 V**, or to a rail whose regulation has not been metered
  under load. `5 V`, `VBUS`, `P+`, and buck output all fail this test until a meter says otherwise.
- **Drive the one-wire pad as a push-pull output.** `setTX()` is `digitalWrite(tx, HIGH);
  pinMode(tx, OUTPUT);` — our end sources current into a line the pack can pull low, which is a
  hard short with nothing but two CMOS on-resistances in it
  ([#99](https://github.com/disruptivepatternmaterial/rak-sensor-node-but-better/issues/99)).
- **Offer a series resistor as protection.** A 1 kΩ resistor was inline when `SDA`/P0.13 died, so
  it is refuted by our own bench, and TI says so independently
  ([#101](https://github.com/disruptivepatternmaterial/rak-sensor-node-but-better/issues/101)).
  The value that would protect collapses the signal against the pack's 15 kΩ pull-down.
- **Fit a replacement core into a harness that has not been cleared.** #102 is explicit: until
  the data-line measurement is in `docs/EVIDENCE.md`, that harness does not touch another core.

## The order of operations, every time

1. **Is the level established?** Look for a measurement in [`../../docs/EVIDENCE.md`](../../docs/EVIDENCE.md)
   or a figure in a datasheet, cited per rule 20. A silkscreen label, a pin name, a forum post,
   and a reference implementation are **none of them** a level.
2. **If not, measure it with the analyzer** — probe on the wire, ground on the source's own
   ground, **node disconnected**, so no pad is exposed while the level is still unknown. The
   procedure for the one-wire line is in
   [`../../docs/HARDWARE.md`](../../docs/HARDWARE.md) § "Qualifying the pack harness".
3. **Record the number** in `docs/EVIDENCE.md` with date, what was probed, and the raw reading.
4. **Only then** name a connection, and cite the measurement when you do.

Step 2 costs a probe clip. Skipping it has cost seven pads and three cores.

## Language that gives the game away

If a sentence about a physical connection contains any of these, the level is not established and
the instruction must not be given:

> "should be", "presumably", "is nominally", "the label says", "the reference design uses",
> "it's a 3.3 V part so", "try connecting", "if that doesn't work, try"

Say instead what is actually true: *"that line's level has never been measured; probe it first."*

## When the operator says an agent told them to do this

Believe them, and treat it as a live defect in the rules rather than a misunderstanding to
correct. Find the instruction in `docs/`, delete or gate it, and file the issue. Do not explain
why the advice was reasonable at the time.

## Citations

- CITE(datasheet): [CIT-NRF-GPIO] nRF52840 Product Specification — absolute maximum on any GPIO
  is `VDD + 0.3 V`, and per-pin absolute maximum current is a damage threshold, not an operating
  point.
- CITE(prior-art): [CIT-NRF-BACKPOWER] Nordic DevZone — "for an unpowered device, max GPIO
  voltage is 0.3 V. Any voltage above this level will make the ESD protection diode conduct and
  you will backpower the device via the GPIO."
- CITE(datasheet): [CIT-SALEAE-LOGICPRO8] [Saleae Logic Pro 8 data sheet](http://downloads.saleae.com/specs/Logic+Pro+8+Data+Sheet.pdf)
  — absolute maximum input −25 V to +25 V, analog input range −10 V to +10 V, input impedance
  2 MΩ ∥ 10 pF, 4.88 mV per LSB.
